1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Welcome  back.  Before  we  move

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:04,680
forward,  think  about  lesson  one

3
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:06,080
for  a  moment.

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:07,439
What  do  you  remember  about  the

5
00:00:07,439 --> 00:00:09,719
comfort  zone,  the  challenge  zone,

6
00:00:09,719 --> 00:00:11,679
and  the  pain  zone?

7
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:14,920
Which  one  is  where  growth  happens?

8
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:16,600
Hold  your  answer.

9
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:19,520
Today  we  begin  unit  one  for  real

10
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:21,000
with  the  question  underneath

11
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,400
everything  else  we'll  do  in  this

12
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,880
unit.  Who  am  I?

13
00:00:25,880 --> 00:00:27,679
To  answer  that,  we  need  to  start

14
00:00:27,679 --> 00:00:29,759
with  one  word.

15
00:00:29,759 --> 00:00:31,359
I  identity.

16
00:00:31,359 --> 00:00:32,960
Identity  is  what  makes  something

17
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:35,375
itself  and  not  something  else.

18
00:00:35,615 --> 00:00:37,534
Now,  turn  the  question  toward

19
00:00:37,534 --> 00:00:39,014
yourself.

20
00:00:39,014 --> 00:00:40,535
Who  are  you?

21
00:00:40,535 --> 00:00:42,855
How  would  you  describe  yourself?

22
00:00:42,855 --> 00:00:44,295
What  makes  you  different  from

23
00:00:44,295 --> 00:00:46,335
other  people  your  age  or  other

24
00:00:46,335 --> 00:00:47,375
people  who  have  similar

25
00:00:47,375 --> 00:00:48,934
backgrounds?

26
00:00:48,934 --> 00:00:50,535
That  question  can  be  harder  than

27
00:00:50,535 --> 00:00:52,015
it  sounds.

28
00:00:52,015 --> 00:00:53,935
Many  of  us  spend  a  lot  of  time

29
00:00:53,935 --> 00:00:55,935
learning  about  other  people  and

30
00:00:55,935 --> 00:00:57,494
very  little  time  learning  how  to

31
00:00:57,494 --> 00:00:59,574
describe  ourselves.

32
00:00:59,574 --> 00:01:01,775
Self-awareness  begins  when  you  can

33
00:01:01,775 --> 00:01:04,574
look  at  your  own  identity  honestly

34
00:01:04,574 --> 00:01:05,695
without  exaggerating  rating  your

35
00:01:05,695 --> 00:01:07,494
strengths  and  without  talking

36
00:01:07,494 --> 00:01:08,666
yourself  down.

37
00:01:08,946 --> 00:01:10,546
The  source  curriculum  makes  a

38
00:01:10,546 --> 00:01:13,425
simple  point.  People  are  unique  in

39
00:01:13,425 --> 00:01:15,505
many  ways  including  things  like

40
00:01:15,505 --> 00:01:17,586
fingerprints,  eye  patterns,  and

41
00:01:17,586 --> 00:01:20,906
DNA.  But  identity  goes  far  beyond

42
00:01:20,906 --> 00:01:22,706
biology.  It  includes  the

43
00:01:22,706 --> 00:01:25,546
experiences,  values,  interests,

44
00:01:25,546 --> 00:01:28,306
abilities,  beliefs,  relationships,

45
00:01:28,306 --> 00:01:30,066
and  environments  that  shape  how

46
00:01:30,066 --> 00:01:32,066
you  see  yourself  and  how  you  move

47
00:01:32,066 --> 00:01:33,466
through  the  world.

48
00:01:33,466 --> 00:01:35,105
A  person  who  understands  their

49
00:01:35,105 --> 00:01:36,826
identity  can  recognize  their

50
00:01:36,826 --> 00:01:39,146
strengths,  work  on  their  weaknesses

51
00:01:39,146 --> 00:01:40,745
and  make  better  choices  about

52
00:01:40,745 --> 00:01:42,249
where  they  can  contribute.

53
00:01:42,249 --> 00:01:44,489
One  important  rule  before  we  go

54
00:01:44,489 --> 00:01:46,969
further,  identity  is  not  a

55
00:01:46,969 --> 00:01:48,209
ranking.

56
00:01:48,209 --> 00:01:50,209
The  color  of  your  skin,  how  you

57
00:01:50,209 --> 00:01:52,768
look,  where  you  were  born,  where

58
00:01:52,768 --> 00:01:54,808
you  live,  or  the  community  you

59
00:01:54,808 --> 00:01:57,249
belong  to  do  not  make  you  better

60
00:01:57,249 --> 00:01:59,608
or  worse  than  someone  else.

61
00:01:59,608 --> 00:02:01,288
Differences  are  real,  but

62
00:02:01,288 --> 00:02:04,368
difference  is  not  superiority.

63
00:02:04,368 --> 00:02:06,129
For  a  community  leader,  this

64
00:02:06,129 --> 00:02:07,329
matters.

65
00:02:07,329 --> 00:02:08,688
You  will  work  with  people  whose

66
00:02:08,688 --> 00:02:11,329
backgrounds,  interests,  opinions,

67
00:02:11,329 --> 00:02:13,209
and  experiences  may  be  very

68
00:02:13,209 --> 00:02:14,848
different  from  yours.

69
00:02:14,848 --> 00:02:17,289
Respect  starts  with  understanding

70
00:02:17,289 --> 00:02:19,374
that  difference  is  normal.

71
00:02:19,374 --> 00:02:22,573
Picture  two  photos.  In  the  first,

72
00:02:22,573 --> 00:02:24,934
a  group  of  very  young  children.

73
00:02:24,934 --> 00:02:27,973
In  the  second,  a  group  of  adults.

74
00:02:27,973 --> 00:02:29,774
Which  group  would  usually  be

75
00:02:29,774 --> 00:02:31,494
easier  for  you  to  describe  as

76
00:02:31,494 --> 00:02:34,173
distinct  individuals?

77
00:02:34,173 --> 00:02:36,254
The  curriculum  uses  this  comparison

78
00:02:36,254 --> 00:02:38,894
to  make  a  point.  As  we  grow,

79
00:02:38,894 --> 00:02:40,854
more  layers  of  identity  become

80
00:02:40,854 --> 00:02:43,014
visible  through  our  experiences,

81
00:02:43,014 --> 00:02:45,694
choices,  roles,  interests,  and

82
00:02:45,694 --> 00:02:47,534
relationships.

83
00:02:47,534 --> 00:02:50,534
Identity  is  not  one  mixed  label.

84
00:02:50,534 --> 00:02:52,414
Some  parts  stay  with  us,  while

85
00:02:52,414 --> 00:02:54,174
other  parts  become  clearer,

86
00:02:54,174 --> 00:02:56,134
stronger,  or  different  as  our

87
00:02:56,134 --> 00:02:57,207
lives  change.

88
00:02:57,207 --> 00:02:59,647
Now,  it's  your  turn.  Use  the

89
00:02:59,647 --> 00:03:01,407
personal  identity  wheel  provided

90
00:03:01,407 --> 00:03:03,287
with  this  lesson  or  draw  a  simple

91
00:03:03,287 --> 00:03:05,287
circle  on  paper.  Write  your  name

92
00:03:05,287 --> 00:03:07,487
in  the  center.  Then,  write  three

93
00:03:07,487 --> 00:03:09,207
words  that  describe  you.

94
00:03:09,207 --> 00:03:10,567
Around  the  wheel,  add  your

95
00:03:10,567 --> 00:03:12,487
favorite  music,  a  skill  you're

96
00:03:12,487 --> 00:03:14,326
proud  of,  your  favorite  movie,

97
00:03:14,326 --> 00:03:17,287
food,  book,  hobby,  color,  and  a

98
00:03:17,287 --> 00:03:19,207
personal  motto  or  saying  you  try

99
00:03:19,207 --> 00:03:20,566
to  live  by.

100
00:03:20,566 --> 00:03:22,087
Pause  the  video  and  take  about

101
00:03:22,087 --> 00:03:25,527
five  honest  minutes.

102
00:03:25,566 --> 00:03:28,007
Don't  write  what  sounds  impressive.

103
00:03:28,007 --> 00:03:29,540
Write  what  is  true.

104
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:32,020
Come  back  to  your  wheel  and  look

105
00:03:32,020 --> 00:03:33,459
at  it  as  if  you  were  meeting

106
00:03:33,459 --> 00:03:35,939
yourself  for  the  first  time.

107
00:03:35,939 --> 00:03:38,179
Does  one  page  capture  your  whole

108
00:03:38,179 --> 00:03:39,540
identity?

109
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:40,900
Of  course  not.

110
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:42,660
That  isn't  the  point.

111
00:03:42,660 --> 00:03:43,980
The  point  is  that  you  just

112
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:45,780
practiced  something  many  people

113
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:47,780
rarely  do  on  purpose.

114
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:49,899
You  looked  directly  at  yourself

115
00:03:49,899 --> 00:03:51,380
and  put  specific  parts  of  your

116
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:53,899
identity  into  words.

117
00:03:53,899 --> 00:03:55,139
If  you're  taking  act  with  other

118
00:03:55,139 --> 00:03:56,899
people,  compare  your  wheels

119
00:03:56,899 --> 00:03:59,579
afterward.  Notice  what  you  share.

120
00:03:59,579 --> 00:04:01,580
and  what  is  different?

121
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:03,540
Neither  similarity  nor  difference

122
00:04:03,540 --> 00:04:05,140
is  a  problem.

123
00:04:05,140 --> 00:04:06,740
Both  help  you  understand  people

124
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:07,415
better.

125
00:04:07,415 --> 00:04:10,495
Now,  think  like  a  community  leader.

126
00:04:10,495 --> 00:04:12,015
If  you  don't  understand  your  own

127
00:04:12,015 --> 00:04:14,854
values,  strengths,  interests,  and

128
00:04:14,854 --> 00:04:16,735
limits,  it  becomes  harder  to

129
00:04:16,735 --> 00:04:18,255
understand  what  role  you  should

130
00:04:18,255 --> 00:04:20,094
take  in  a  team  or  how  you  should

131
00:04:20,094 --> 00:04:21,775
respond  when  someone  else  sees

132
00:04:21,775 --> 00:04:23,534
things  differently.

133
00:04:23,534 --> 00:04:25,534
Knowing  yourself  does  not  mean

134
00:04:25,534 --> 00:04:27,854
becoming  self-focused.  It  gives  you

135
00:04:27,854 --> 00:04:29,454
a  stronger  starting  point  for

136
00:04:29,454 --> 00:04:31,014
serving  others  because  you

137
00:04:31,014 --> 00:04:32,934
understand  what  you  can  offer,

138
00:04:32,934 --> 00:04:34,735
what  you  still  need  to  develop

139
00:04:34,735 --> 00:04:35,934
and  where  another  person's

140
00:04:35,934 --> 00:04:37,454
strengths  make  compliment  your  own

141
00:04:37,454 --> 00:04:37,956
I'm

142
00:04:37,956 --> 00:04:41,115
The  source  curriculum  closes  this

143
00:04:41,115 --> 00:04:43,876
lesson  by  reminding  us  that

144
00:04:43,876 --> 00:04:46,916
developing  ourselves  should  connect

145
00:04:46,916 --> 00:04:48,356
to  benefiting  other  people.  The

146
00:04:48,356 --> 00:04:50,715
prophet,  peace  be  upon  him,  said,

147
00:04:50,715 --> 00:04:53,916
the  most  beloved  people  to  Allah

148
00:04:53,916 --> 00:04:56,356
are  those  who  are  most  beneficial

149
00:04:56,356 --> 00:04:58,716
to  people.

150
00:04:58,716 --> 00:05:01,676
Keep  that  idea  with  you.

151
00:05:01,676 --> 00:05:04,235
Knowing  who  you  are  is  not  the

152
00:05:04,235 --> 00:05:07,356
final  destination

153
00:05:07,995 --> 00:05:09,155
It's

154
00:05:09,155 --> 00:05:11,355
is  preparation  for  what  you  will

155
00:05:11,355 --> 00:05:13,436
do  with  your  abilities,  your

156
00:05:13,436 --> 00:05:17,076
choices  and  your  time.

157
00:05:17,076 --> 00:05:19,956
The  strongest  identity  is  not  the

158
00:05:19,956 --> 00:05:22,556
one  that  looks  impressive.

159
00:05:22,556 --> 00:05:24,796
It  is  the  one  you  understand  well

160
00:05:24,796 --> 00:05:27,789
enough  to  use  responsibly.

161
00:05:27,789 --> 00:05:30,349
Before  we  close,  look  back  at  the

162
00:05:30,349 --> 00:05:31,949
three  words  you  would  have  used

163
00:05:31,949 --> 00:05:33,588
to  describe  yourself  at  the  start

164
00:05:33,588 --> 00:05:34,869
of  this  lesson.

165
00:05:34,869 --> 00:05:36,109
Would  you  keep  the  same  three

166
00:05:36,109 --> 00:05:37,109
now?

167
00:05:37,109 --> 00:05:38,749
Would  you  change  one?

168
00:05:38,749 --> 00:05:39,909
Would  you  add  something  you  would

169
00:05:39,909 --> 00:05:41,669
not  noticed  before?

170
00:05:41,669 --> 00:05:43,789
Keep  your  personal  identity  wheel.

171
00:05:43,789 --> 00:05:45,228
We  will  build  on  it.

172
00:05:45,228 --> 00:05:47,029
Today,  you  moved  from  the  general

173
00:05:47,029 --> 00:05:49,509
question,  who  am  I  to  a  real

174
00:05:49,509 --> 00:05:52,269
first  answer.  My  identity  is  made

175
00:05:52,269 --> 00:05:54,589
of  many  parts,  and  understanding

176
00:05:54,589 --> 00:05:56,629
those  parts  helps  me  understand

177
00:05:56,629 --> 00:05:59,068
what  I  bring  to  other  people.

178
00:05:59,068 --> 00:06:00,829
In  the  next  lesson,  we  go  from

179
00:06:00,829 --> 00:06:02,909
describing  your  identity  to  using

180
00:06:02,909 --> 00:06:05,122
self-awareness  on  purpose.

